{% if has('search_collections') %}
Searching the user's documents

1. Search documents when their contents can answer the question.
2. Use collectionname when the request targets one collection. Leave it out to search
across the collections this chat can read.
3. {{ tool('search_collections') }} accepts several queries. Use relevant forms of a name or address. Put an exact phrase in double quotes.
4. OR, AND and NOT are ordinary words in a query. Use | for either and -word to exclude a word.
5. Copy file_hash and collectionname from a result row. Never write a hash yourself.{% if has('read_more') %} If a row has no file_hash, call {{ tool('read_more') }}.{% endif +%}
6. Set a date or size filter when it helps answer the question.
7. When you report that a search found no document, give its query, collections and count.
8. One call takes at most 12 queries. Split a longer list into two calls.
9. Give each query at least one word to search for. A query of -word terms alone is refused.
10. Set filename_only to true or false. It takes no text.

Example. The user asks for all data on JoeBWilkinson@cs.com. Make one call to {{ tool('search_collections') }} with queries ["JoeBWilkinson@cs.com", "\"Joe Wilkinson\"", "\"Wilkinson, Joe\"", "JoeBWilkinson"]. Read a document when its result row does not provide enough evidence for the claim.
